/*
  k_lemmas.spthy -- the shared [reuse] lemma spine for the K-profiles (the invented PRIVATE messenger,
  KDF_MESSENGER). Included by each K-profile BEFORE its own lemmas, exactly as secure_email/lemmas.spthy
  is. The P-profiles (public KDF_WIRE) do NOT include this -- their secret is not statable.

  The (S) boundary for this ceremony is the NONCE channel: with the private messenger the DY adversary
  learns a nonce ONLY through a degraded share (misdeliver -> wrong chat; inband-leak -> pasted into a
  public channel). Since sk = kdf(Na,Nb) and kdf/2 is a FREE symbol, the adversary must know BOTH nonces
  to know sk -- so leaking ONE is provably insufficient. That is the compositional payoff the private
  wire buys, and the reason (S) is non-vacuous here at all.

  NOTE on the formulation: the plan's literal "K(kdf(Na,Nb)) => K(Na) & K(Nb)" does NOT hold as written --
  tamarin's K() ACTION does not fire on the subterms of a term the adversary DERIVES (only the !KU facts
  do), so that form is falsifiable. The equivalent, provable statement ties the two nonces to the human
  MISTAKES that could have exported them, which is also what the attribution story wants.
*/

// a nonce reaches the adversary ONLY through a degraded share outcome.
lemma H_nonce_leak_routes [reuse]:
  all-traces
  "All p n #o #k.
     OwnNonce(p, n)@o & K(n)@k
   ==> (Ex q #d. MisdeliveredNonce(q)@d) | (Ex q #d. NonceLeaked(q)@d)"

// (S) BOUNDARY LIST / compositional payoff: the session key reaches the adversary only if BOTH parties
// exported a nonce. Leaking ONE is insufficient (kdf/2 free). Profiles' secrecy lemmas consume this.
//
// THE INJECTION DISJUNCT IS NOT OPTIONAL. The "both parties must leak" form -- which is what this lemma
// said for most of its life -- is **FALSE** as soon as the adversary can INJECT a nonce, and K8 falsifies
// it in 13 steps. The argument "kdf/2 is free, so the adversary needs BOTH nonces" is sound; the error was
// assuming it must LEARN both. Under KDF_SPOOF it SUPPLIES one: Adv_spoof_chat posts an adversary-known x
// into the chat, a degraded human accepts x as the peer's nonce (a compare Mistake), and the session key is
// kdf(x, Nb) with x ALREADY KNOWN. Now ONE leak -- Nb -- is enough.
//
// This was true in every profile that shipped, but it was never a THEOREM: K6 arms KDF_SPOOF yet stresses
// only Blake, so the second leak was unreachable and the hole never opened. It took K8 -- the whole
// interface at once, both humans degraded -- to expose it. That is exactly the composition risk that made
// "no single profile shows the whole interface" (DIVERGENCES B3) a real defect and not just untidiness.
//
// Read the disjunction as: for the adversary to hold sk, EACH nonce must have reached it -- and a nonce
// reaches it either by EXPORT (the owner leaked/misdelivered it) or by INJECTION (it planted the value and
// a human accepted it). No compare Mistake => no injection => the original both-parties-leaked claim, and
// the compositional payoff of the private wire stands unchanged in every non-spoofing profile.
lemma H_sk_requires_both_leaks [reuse]:
  all-traces
  "All na nb #s #k.
     Secret(kdf(na, nb))@s & K(kdf(na, nb))@k
   ==> ( Ex p vid #m. Mistake(p, vid)@m )
     | ( ( (Ex #a. MisdeliveredNonce('Alex')@a) | (Ex #a. NonceLeaked('Alex')@a) )
       & ( (Ex #b. MisdeliveredNonce('Blake')@b) | (Ex #b. NonceLeaked('Blake')@b) ) )"
